  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The butter-foot bolete (Boletus auripes) is a medium-sized fungus belonging to the boletes family, characterized by its distinctive golden-yellow stem base and brown cap. It is commonly found throughout New England and is edible, though not considered choice for culinary use.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Golden-yellow stem base",
      "description": "The most distinctive feature is the bright golden-yellow or buttery coloration at the base of the stem, which fades to pale yellow or whitish toward the top.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Brown cap with pores",
      "description": "The cap is typically brown and somewhat flat, with small yellow pores underneath that may bruise blue when pressed.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flesh characteristics",
      "description": "The flesh is pale yellow and may show blue staining when cut, particularly near the base of the stem.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Butter-foot boletes grow on the forest floor in mixed hardwood and conifer forests, preferring areas with rich soil and appearing from summer through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The scientific name 'auripes' literally means 'golden-foot,' directly referencing the striking yellow coloration at the base of the stem that makes this species so easy to identify.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
  "contextualKnowledge": null,
